ces are or used to be the  interesting stuff for sure but what does  it have to do with me oh well my friend  as you may have heard I recently kicked  off a new series of videos where I  covered the lore behind every dungeon  from various Zelda titles so how's about  you and I go on a little dungeon expert  Edition together and you can help split  the effort 50 50. I'm sure I guess  magnificent oh uh can I have the Shadow  Temple yes you can have the Shadow  Temple nice alright then let's get  started  [Music]  ah yes the Deku Tree a true Zelda  Classic if there ever was one of course  in terms of gameplay it's nothing to  really write home about I mean it  features some of the most basic puzzles  in the series and you can easily Breeze  through it in under 20 minutes or so  even on a first playthrough but keep in  mind that this was the first 3D Zelda  dungeon ever conceived and navigating a  space in three dimensions was still a  bit of a novel concept at the time yes  even something as simple as opening  doors back in the old days you just  walked into a door and Pat a bang bad  boom you're in another room but now you  have to press a button I don't know what  to do but what about the lore behind  this place well I mean it's inside of  the Deku Tree no mystery there but  there's definitely some ambiguity in  history here the origin of the Deku Tree  is in large part left up to the  imagination of the player we know he's a  guardian Spirit one of many seen across  the series and that he's been around for  a very long time popping up in various  places across Zelda chronology all the  way up to breath of the wild which as  many of you know takes place somewhere  at the end of the timeline that said  Ocarina of Time is his first appearance  both in real life as well as in Universe  has no game taking place before Ocarina  features the Deku Tree as we know it  there is however a potentially distant  relative to be found in Skyward Sword  the very first Adventure in the  chronology in the Faron Woods area  there's a gigantic tree towering over  the entire Forest aptly named The Great  tree unsurprisingly many suspect that  this is the great Deku Tree in its  primordial form so to speak before he  became a sentient being who watches over  the forest and its inhabitants its size  and location roughly match that of the  great Deku Tree and just like the forest  Guardian from Ocarina of Time the great  tree is hollow on the inside there's a  central chamber with a similar looking  pattern that spirals upwards as well as  a tunnel leading to a basement like  space below the root system of the gray  tree it's kind of crazy to think that  this could very well be the same place  the hero of time would be put to the  test many ages later so what about the  dungeon part of him I mean it's made  obvious that the Deku Tree has always  been Hollow on the inside but what's up  with all the architecture you know the  doors ladders torches and all these  other Contraptions and not to mention  the monster infestation it's explained  that right before the start of Link's  Adventure Ganondorf himself actually  paid a visit to the Deku Tree in person  his goal was to press the guardian of  the forest into giving him the Kokiri  Emerald one of three spiritual Stones  needed to open the door to the sacred  realm and claim the Triforce when the  Deku Tree refused Ganondorf placed a  curse on him out of spite not cool  Ganondorf not cool at all agreed poor  Deku Tree by the time link is summoned  to meet the forest Guardian the curse  has allegedly been festering inside him  for quite some time link is then tasked  with breaking the curse by venturing  inside as a way to test his wisdom and  courage to see if he is ready to face  the dangers ahead and ultimately save  Hyrule now you would think that this  explains the various monsters roaming  around the tree's innards but actually  it doesn't well not entirely anyway  while there are some monsters who could  be considered responsible for the slow  decline line of the Deku tree's Health  like the Goma who are described as  parasitic there are various creatures  here who don't seem to have anything to  do with the curse most notably the Deku  scrubs one strange detail is that for  some reason the scrubs refer to Goma as  their Queen which is kind of odd when  you think about it since they're not  even remotely the same species we do  find creatures who are of the same  species and even share the same name a  larval State version of Goma who would  no doubt grow to the same size if given  enough time it makes you wonder whether  or not the Goma appeared after the curse  was casted or if they've always lived  here undisturbed in that ganondorf's  curse either made them more parasitic  and destructive to their environment or  may even share no connection to the  curse at all the Deku Tree doesn't  exactly do a good job at explaining why  he's dying all he says is that the curse  is sapping his power but anyway after  beating the dungeon it's also revealed  that while Link's efforts to break the  curse were Valiant the fate of the great  Deku Tree was already sealed and he knew  this so as stated before it really was  all just a test for the hero to prove  himself as opposed to an attempt save  the giant guardian's Life As for the  interior design of the tree itself there  are a few different options as to why it  looks the way it does because it's a  video game level made for humans by  humans for the sole purpose of  entertainment no well okay yeah but come  on man this is a Zelda Theory Let's  ignore the cold-hearted reality for a  moment here anyway the most obvious  answer would be that since it was a test  for the hero it is just that a trial  conceived by the forest Guardian perhaps  built by the Deku scrubs or the Kokiri  so that one day The Chosen hero would be  able to enter and prove his worthiness a  solid enough explanation though that is  some impressive bit of foresight on the  side of the Deku Tree but whatever I  mean he is an ancient Spirit Guardian I  guess another option as to why the Deku  trees insides have man-made features to  them is that it served as a kind of  shelter a place for the beings of the  forest to retreat to in case the forest  would ever come under siege even for  Deku scrubs apparently so clearly tree  man over here doesn't discriminate in  breath of the wild we can see a similar  occurrence where some of the four  Spirits have made a home inside inside  the Deku Tree setting up shops and  living spaces and it makes sense the  Deku Tree is a being who provides safety  and comfort he's a guardian who protects  the forest and its children from the  dangers lurking outside  [Music]  Jabo jabo's belly perhaps better known  as the grossest dungeon in Zelda history  again no mystery in regards to what this  place is it's the insides of Lord  jabu-jabu the only fish in history to  receive lordship as far as I'm aware and  one with some major digestive problems  like I said grossest Zelda dungeon ever  the situation surrounding jabu-jabu is  actually quite similar to that of the  Deku Tree he's a guardian in this case  of the Zora who got cursed by Ganondorf  after the Zora King refused to hand over  their spiritual Stone the Zora Sapphire  here too the curse takes on the form of  a kind of illness something which is  slowly festering and corrupting the  Creature from the inside if you pay  close attention you can even hear that  jabu's heartbeat is integrated into the  music track and purposely sounds  irregular and offbeat  foreign  Princess Ruto explains that she tends to  jabu-jabu on a regular basis and as such  the guardian and her share a close Bond  but that one day presumably after he was  cursed jabu-jabu swallowed the princess  hole along with her most treasured  belonging the Zora Sapphire the same one  Ganondorf was trying to obtain from the  Zora King now it may seem that Ruto  getting munched up by her own fish God  was a one-time anomaly caused by the  sickness It suffers from but Ruto  mentions that she's actually been  venturing inside jabu's big old belly  ever since she was little for what  reason we don't know and is perhaps best  left unexplained the only difference is  that this time the inside of jabu-jabu  has become a lot more hazardous Ruto  mentions the sudden appearance of  electric jellyfish as well as strange  holes which allegedly weren't there  before a clear indication that  ganondorf's death curse is slowly eating  away at the Zora Guardian from within  which can I just say is one of the most  disturbing Fates I could possibly  imagine yeah man Ganondorf doesn't mess  around so after traversing the dungeon's  death link is similarly faced with a  giant parasitic creature and the alleged  Source behind jabu-jabu suffering the  bioelectric anemone anemone baronade no  doubt named after the real life cm in  Menominee some of which are parasitic to  other marine life however just like the  great Deku Tree jabu jabu's death may  have been imminent as well despite  Link's best efforts the reason being is  that when link travels seven years into  the future the Zora Guardian is nowhere  to be found it's actually one of the  more famous mysteries in Ocarina of Time  and one which a lot of people have  speculated about over the years some  think he may have survived and was  relocated somewhere else others say he  probably died either from the curse or  the rapid changing climate in and around  Zora's domain unfortunately nozora ever  explains his absence or when and why he  disappeared it is true that a creature  similar to jabu-jabu named jaboon makes  an appearance in the Wind Waker which  takes place in the same timeline but  aside from the similar name it's hard to  say if they are actually related by  blood in any way if they are there is a  chance that jabu-jabu did survive the  curse and the freezing of the domain but  if they are not related then perhaps  jabun was already born before the curse  was placed on jabu-jabu I'd say that he  most likely died while link was in  purgatory for seven years the fact that  the Deku Tree still wasn't able to  survive the curse despite the  eradication of Goma doesn't bode well  for jabujabu I mean after all they are  similar in stature that is to say  Guardian Spirits it's also worth  mentioning that even after destroying  baronade the inside of jabu-jabu doesn't  change or heal the strange holes in  electric jellyfish are still everywhere  whatever happened to jabu-jabu during  and after the events of Ocarina of Time  others like him have picked up the torch  in the future however his disappearance  from the adult timeline will likely  remain one of Ocarina of time's many  inexplicable Mysteries  the fire temple everyone's not favorite  but also not least favorite Zelda  dungeon generally speaking of course  it's one of those dungeons that isn't  really talked about all that much  compared to some of the others and I can  see why it doesn't have the memorable  atmosphere or sense of mystery I say the  forest or Shadow Temple it doesn't have  the killer soundtrack of the spirit  Temple nor does it have the infamous  reputation of the water temple as gen Z  would say it's kind of mid the only  things that really stand out about the  fire temple are the ICONic final boss  because you know who doesn't enjoy  fighting a giant dragon and of course  the famous controversy surrounding the  Temple's original music track which  includes recordings of actual  translatable Islamic chants which  Nintendo was unaware of at the time  foreign  [Music]  as such the music was preemptively  changed and the original score now  exists only in the 1.0 and 1.1 builds of  the game although the change is  perfectly understandable the inclusion  of chanting was actually quite fitting  similar to the water and spirit Temple  the fire temple was considered to have  been a place of worship for the gorons  before it was corrupted by Ganondorf as  evident from the many altars and statues  present here there's also a few hints  and remnants of that of a prison like  the holding cells that housed some of  the captured gorons but it's unclear if  these were always here or if they were  constructed after the temple was already  corrupted we don't know much about the  fire Temple's construction but it  possibly had something to do with the  famous Goron Legend darunia Sun speaks  of the story of an unnamed Goron hero  who single-handedly defeated an ancient  fire dragon using a giant hammer a  battle that saved the Goron race from  Extinction the current day leader of the  gorons darunia is even said to be a  descendant of said hero needless to say  this was a major historical event in  Goron culture and could have been the  Catalyst for the construction of the  fire temple in the distant past both as  a way to commemorate the Goron hero of  old as well as to enshrine the final  resting place of their deceased  adversary volvagia even the legendary  Hammer used to defeat the dragon is  still kept on an altar deep inside the  temple after ganondorf's rise to power  in Hyrule he not only corrupted the fire  temple but the self-proclaimed King of  Evil resurrected the ancient dragon to  punish and eradicate the gorons for  defying him the sad part about the story  is that when link arrives at the fire  temple darunia himself decides to face  volvagia alone in order to buy links  some time to free his imprisoned people  however as he himself states he does not  possess the legendary Hammer yet still  decides to go in despite the odds I'm  sure you can tell where we're going with  this but there's long since been a  debate on whether or not the sages are  actually dead or not and darunia's  heroic sacrifice is often used as  evidence that they very well could be as  opposed to the other sages whose fate is  kept a bit more ambiguous in the case of  darunia it's pretty evident as to what  happened to him I mean we see him  entering volvagia's Lair all by himself  yet we never see him come out out alive  and by the time link follows suit  volvagia is still there ready to rock  and roll meanwhile darunia is nowhere to  be found the fire temple may not be the  most popular or most memorable dungeon  in Ocarina of Time but it definitely  features an engaging story a story of  sacrifice of bravery and an ancient evil  brought back from the afterlife as one  of the more iconic Final Bosses in the  series  [Music]  oh boy here we go this Shadow Temple or  as I like to call it the E for everyone  dungeon yeah Nintendo definitely wasn't  holding back with this one now it's not  necessarily the most popular dungeon  when it comes to gameplay in part due to  its extreme linearity but it is one of  the most talked about locations when it  comes to lore it fully Embraces the  darker and more mature side of the  series but does so in a way that most of  it is left up to the imagination of the  player we learned that prior to the  events of Ocarina of Time Hyrule was  embroiled in a Fierce and gruesome Civil  War the same war that made link an  orphan killing his father and forcing  his mortally wounded mother to leave the  boy in the care of the great Deku Tree  before she herself passed away as well  the hyrulean Civil War as it's called  would ultimately decide which of the  four prominent races would gain control  over the land of Hyrule which after an  era of Bloodshed the hylians ended up  winning with the remaining factions  swearing loyalty to the Hylian royal  family when Link's Adventure Starts the  war has only been resolved for a few  years and the Shadow Temple is a remnant  of some of the Hidden crew that took  place at the time it was built by the  sheikah a tribe who has long served the  royal family of Hyrule both in peacetime  as well as during the war it's revealed  that they served as a sort of secret  police force who operated from the  Shadows serving as Royal bodyguards  assassinating enemies of the king as  well as taking care of prisoners of War  locking them up and even resorting to  torture to get information until  eventually disposing of them in ways  that are perhaps better left imagined of  course this wasn't exactly something the  royal family wanted the world to see and  was preferably kept from the history  books and so below the depths of  kakuriko Village home of the sheikah at  the time of the war the Shadow Temple  was used to obscure these acts of  Cruelty and keep them out of sight from  the public eye this place represents one  of the darkest depths Nintendo has ever  dared to go with their storytelling even  if it is hidden under the surface both  literally and figuratively interestingly  enough although the Shadow Temple is one  of the more popular dungeons to theorize  about in many respects it has the most  fleshed out backstory out of all five  temples present in Ocarina of Time we  know exactly what this place is and the  purpose it once served a secret wartime  prison and torture site for enemies of  Hyrule that isn't to say that there are  no Mysteries left to unravel about the  Shadow Temple though there's the  mysterious floating ship that carries  its passengers across a foggy Chasm for  example very reminiscent of the undead  fairy man who carries Souls across the  river sticks we still don't know for  sure why it's here or what it was used  for though there are plenty of theories  about it of course another interesting  feature are the many statues of what  seem to be blind rats or mice perhaps a  reference to the act of turning a blind  eye to the atrocities committed on  behalf of the royal family it's a  central theme all throughout the Shadow  Temple the aspect of the Unseen Hidden  Truths and events taking place in the  shadows this is exemplified by the lens  of Truth also known as The Eye of Truth  a magic artifact capable of peering  through and revealing the unseen even  the Insignia of the shika itself is  representative of this as well as the  symbol on the door of the Shadow Temple  of an eye in three stages open open half  open and close and of course we can't  talk about the Shadow Temple without  also mentioning Bongo Bongo you know  just another piece of nightmare fuel  strike from the minds of the Zelda team  We're Not Gonna dive into the possible  identity of Bongo Bongo as there are so  many theories about it it would take  ages to get through all of them besides  I happen to have already made a video  that focuses specifically on the bosses  of Ocarina of Time but a general  consensus is that he could have been one  of the prisoners who was maimed and  killed in the Shadow Temple by the  sheikah or perhaps the concoction of  many of them turning them or him into a  monstrous evil spirit being hell-bent on  causing destruction mainly due to his  appearance which is that of a torso with  a missing head and amputated hands with  all that said it's possible that the  Shadow Temple once served a different  purpose before it was repurposed into a  torture Prison from Hell perhaps just  like some of the other temples it was  once a place of worship or maybe  something akin to a mausoleum a place  where the dead were laid to rest which  would explain the floating ship as it  could have been used for a sort of  soul-fearing ritual it's not even clear  if ganondorf's influence had anything to  do with the current state of the Shadow  Temple though his corruption has spread  on a Country-Wide scale it's arguable if  the Shadow Temple had any need of an  outside force to corrupt it the history  of this place alone seems to have been  perfectly capable of causing a  Corruption of its own and some areas in  and around kakarika were already  Infested by the undead long before  ganondorf's power grab with the Shadow  Temple Nintendo truly walked the line  between a relatively innocent and  classic fantasy story and straight up  horror and it's unlikely that we'll ever  see something even close to it in the  meantime though it'll remain a hidden  part of hyrule's bloody history and  theories about it will no doubt continue  to be discussed for many years to come  and that my friends concludes this part  of the lore behind the dungeons in  Ocarina of Time be sure to let us know  your thoughts and theories on some of  these locations down in the comments and  of course go and check out the other  half on my friend Bandit's Channel which  covers the dangers Cavern the forest  water and spirit Temple anyway thank you  for the collaboration good sir anytime  Mr Mays you you know me I hear the words  occurring of time and I jump in head  first no matter whose Channel or video  it is so um yeah thanks for uh yeah  thanks for having me I had a lot of fun  and I'm so grateful for your audience  for putting up with me as well if you  guys watching are interested in the  other dungeons like Mr May said they are  live right now over on my channel so hey  maybe see you there Bandit away  and with that I bid you all farewell and  I hope you have a great week a special  thanks to Bandit games to my mods and of  course my patreons and channel members I  hope to see you all in the next video  this is Don signing off and have a good  one  foreign  [Music]  [Music]

